**Read:** [Physical chemistry of interfaces and colloids — Diethelm Johannsmann](../sources/Physical-Chemistry-of-Interfaces-and-Colloids-Johannsmann.lingua#r_5FAT1MRFSVXVDS9Z6VCF4YM7TD), section 10.7; [Particle removal and MEMS damage — Hirano and colleagues](../sources/Particle-Removal-and-MEMS-Damage-Hirano.lingua#r_0RH4K2MTZZG42NZ4MMZE1K6G9E), the role assigned to surface interactions in its particular cleaning comparison.

An electrophoretic measurement gives velocity relative to fluid per applied electric field. Inferring zeta potential requires an electrokinetic model. In the thin-double-layer, weak-field Smoluchowski limit, mobility µep≈εζ/η. Here ζ belongs to an idealized hydrodynamic slip plane and is not automatically the potential at the chemical surface.

Geometry, double-layer thickness, surface conduction, polarization and suspension conditions can change the inference. A value measured on dispersed powder in one liquid may not describe an attached particle on a processed wafer in another liquid. The hypothetical mobility below corresponds to approximately −28.81 mV within the stated model.

**Work:** Convert a hypothetical mobility −2×10⁻⁸ m²/(V s), η=10⁻³ Pa s and ε=78.4ε0 to ζ, checking its sign and units. State the required ratio between particle size and screening length. Design a comparison that controls pH and ionic composition while separating altered electrostatic interaction from a changed removal flow.

